  "acceptance": "This record covers the CPU timing control. The supplied app-local DXVK game binaries remain the physically accepted game configuration. The change has not been accepted for live gameplay.",
  "observed_tradeoff": {
    "comparisons": 12,
    "lower_thread_cpu_share": 12,
    "larger_p95_absolute_period_error": 8,
    "decision": "Retain the existing game limiter. The bounded-spin candidate reduced measured thread CPU in every setting, but several timing tails worsened. On ARM64EC at 8 Hz, P95 absolute period error increased from 3.7164 ms to 90.2586 ms across the two short runs.",
    "accounting": "GetThreadTimes has finite accounting granularity. One x64 group reported zero charged time despite nonzero thread-cycle counts. No system-wide CPU or power saving is inferred."
  },
  "high_resolution_timer_trial": {
    "candidate_change": "Use a high-resolution waitable timer owned by the calling thread. After a confirmed timer creation, cap the extra spin margin at one timer quantum. If the Windows timer cannot be created or a wait fails, use the original system wait path.",
    "measurement_method": "Two counterbalanced pairs per architecture. Three warm-ups for each cap precede the local QPC samples. Thread CPU is kernel plus user time from GetThreadTimes, as a percentage of one logical processor. P95 is the absolute distance from the requested interval. No graphics context or game was created by the timing program.",
    "probe_logging": "The two timing builds increment one per-thread test counter after each completed blocking wait. The production patch excludes these counters. Waits inside the spin loop are not instrumented.",
    "probe_source_sha256": "a02bfb1858d9d8725ac75210689258efa1945dbead80fcf81465ee639c3ec223",
